/*
 * Boot order (crates/shep-core/src/config/graph.rs for the sort,
 * crates/shep-daemon/src/boot_order.rs for the driver that runs it,
 * crates/shep-core/src/config/app.rs for the depends_on field itself and
 * crates/shep-core/src/config/daemon.rs for boot_first_dogs). Every
 * transcript below was run against target/release/shep on 2026-09-06.
 */
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import ReferencePills from "../../components/docs/ReferencePills.astro";
import Callout from "../../components/docs/Callout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/docs/CodeBlock.astro";

interface GraphRow {
  problem: string;
  typed: string;
  boot: string;
}

const graphRows: GraphRow[] = [
  {
    problem: "A cycle",
    typed: "Refused, exit 4, naming the cycle as a path",
    boot: "Warned about; those sheep start last, in no particular order",
  },
  {
    problem: "A name the flock does not have",
    typed: "Warned about; the edge is satisfied and the sheep starts",
    boot: "Warned about; the edge is satisfied and the sheep starts",
  },
  {
    problem: "A dependency with autostart = false",
    typed: "Warned about; it stays stopped and the sheep starts",
    boot: "Warned about; it stays stopped and the sheep starts",
  },
  {
    problem: "A self-edge, or a name:slot target",
    typed: "Refused, exit 4, before anything is registered",
    boot: "Refused; the entry lands in the rejected list",
  },
];

const stagedStart = `$ shep start Flockfile.toml
ID  NAME  STATUS  PID    RESTARTS  EXIT  CFG  CPU  MEM   UPTIME  FOLD  SMIT
1   api   online  21899  0         -     -    -    1.2M  3s      -     -
0   db    online  21886  0         -     -    -    1.2M  6s      -     -
2   web   online  21907  0         -     -    -    1.2M  0s      -     -`;

const probedStart = `$ shep start probed.toml
ID  NAME  STATUS  PID    RESTARTS  EXIT  CFG  CPU  MEM   UPTIME  FOLD  SMIT
4   api   online  21994  0         -     -    -    1.2M  0s      -     -
3   db    online  21993  0         -     -    -    9.5M  0s      -     -`;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Boot order"
  description="depends_on names what a sheep waits for. shep sorts the flock into stages and starts them in order."
  crumb="Concepts / Boot order"
  activeSlug="boot-order"
>
  <article>
    <h1>Boot order</h1>
    <ReferencePills slug="boot-order" />
    <p class="lede">
      shep starts a flock all at once unless you tell it otherwise. Add
      <code>depends_on</code> to an app and it sorts the flock into stages
      instead, holding each one until its members are up. An app that would
      have crash-looped through its restart budget waiting for a database
      waits for the database instead.
    </p>

    <h2>Naming a dependency</h2>
    <p>
      <code>depends_on</code> is a list of other sheep, or of dogs. It goes
      in the app's own Flockfile, because a requirement the app has belongs
      in the app's own repository.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock label="Flockfile.toml">{`[[app]]
name   = "db"
script = "./db"

[[app]]
name       = "api"
script     = "./api"
depends_on = ["db"]

[[app]]
name       = "web"
script     = "./web"
depends_on = ["api"]`}</CodeBlock>
    <p>
      That sorts into three stages, <code>db</code>, then <code>api</code>,
      then <code>web</code>. Names inside a stage are sorted, so one flock
      always plans the same way. The whole start took 6.17 seconds, and the
      <code>UPTIME</code> column is where it went:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock>{stagedStart}</CodeBlock>
    <p class="fine">
      Names only. <code>web:2</code> is refused with the app-level form
      named, since a dependency on one instance of a load-balanced app is
      not a claim about availability. A dependency on a multi-instance app
      waits for every instance of it.
    </p>

    <h2>What ready means, and what it costs</h2>
    <p>
      A stage advances when its members have settled. An app with
      <code>wait_ready</code> or a <code>readiness_probe</code> settles the
      moment it says so, and an app that exits settles at once rather than
      holding its stage for the full wait.
    </p>
    <p>
      An app with neither has nothing to report. shep holds it
      <code>starting</code> for its own <code>listen_timeout</code>, three
      seconds by default, then calls it up and moves on. That is where the
      6.17 seconds went: <code>db</code> cost one wait and <code>api</code>
      cost another. <code>web</code> is last, so nothing waits on it and it
      costs nothing. Only apps something depends on pay this.
    </p>
    <p>
      Configuring a real signal takes it back off. Two apps, the database
      behind a TCP probe polled every hundred milliseconds, and the start
      finishes in 0.14 seconds:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock label="probed.toml">{`[[app]]
name            = "db"
script          = "./db"
readiness_probe = { kind = "tcp", target = "127.0.0.1:5432", interval = "100ms" }

[[app]]
name       = "api"
script     = "./api"
depends_on = ["db"]`}</CodeBlock>
    <CodeBlock>{probedStart}</CodeBlock>
    <Callout variant="careful">
      That <code>interval</code> is doing real work. A probe polls every ten
      seconds by default and the first poll runs at spawn, so an app needing
      fifty milliseconds to bind its port fails that first poll and has
      nothing left to answer with before <code>listen_timeout</code>
      elapses. Measured on the flock above: 0.14 seconds at an interval of
      100ms, and the full 3.05 at the default. Set an interval shorter than
      the app's own startup time or the probe buys you nothing at boot.
    </Callout>

    <h2>Dogs run last, unless you move one</h2>
    <p>
      A dog is a node in the same graph, and by default it lands in a final
      stage after every sheep. That is the right place for the metrics dog:
      it must not answer for a flock that is not up yet.
    </p>
    <p>
      A log-rotation dog is the opposite case, since it has to be running
      before a sheep starts writing. Name it in
      <code>[daemon] boot_first_dogs</code> and it runs ahead of everything:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock label="$SHEP_HOME/shep.toml">{`[daemon]
enabled_dogs    = ["metrics", "log-rotate"]
boot_first_dogs = ["log-rotate"]`}</CodeBlock>
    <CodeBlock label="the resulting boot">{`stage 0  log-rotate
stage 1  db
stage 2  api
stage 3  web
stage 4  metrics`}</CodeBlock>
    <p class="fine">
      A name here that is not in <code>enabled_dogs</code> is inert: nothing
      enables a dog by promoting it. A sheep may also name a dog in its own
      <code>depends_on</code>, but at a boot that does not move the dog. The
      shepherd spawns dogs in two groups, the promoted ones before the
      restore and the rest after it, and neither group sits at a stage
      boundary. A sheep depending on an unpromoted dog is warned about and
      started anyway. <code>boot_first_dogs</code> is the lever that
      actually reorders one.
    </p>

    <h2>When the graph is wrong</h2>
    <p>
      What happens depends on who is watching. An operator typing
      <code>shep start</code> or <code>shep add</code> is there to fix a
      typo. A machine rebooting at 4am is not, and stranding its whole flock
      over one is worse than starting it in a bad order.
    </p>
    <div class="field-table">
      <div class="row header">
        <span class="col-name">Problem</span>
        <span class="col-does">shep start, shep add</span>
        <span class="col-does">A boot, or shep muster</span>
      </div>
      {
        graphRows.map((r) => (
          <div class="row">
            <span class="col-name">{r.problem}</span>
            <span class="col-does">{r.typed}</span>
            <span class="col-does">{r.boot}</span>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
    <p>
      The cycle refusal names the actual cycle, not the set of apps caught
      in one, because a path is the thing you can break:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock>{`$ shep start cycle.toml
error[invalid_config]: the daemon reported InvalidConfig: dependency cycle: api -> db -> cache -> api`}</CodeBlock>
    <p class="fine">
      The graph it checks spans the batch and the registered flock together,
      so a cycle can close through a sheep the file never mentions. Only a
      cycle this batch is part of refuses it; a knot standing elsewhere in
      the registry is left to whichever request drew it.
    </p>
    <p>
      An unknown name warns rather than refusing on both sides. A dependency
      on an app whose Flockfile lives in another repository is legitimate,
      and refusing it would make cross-repository dependencies unusable.
    </p>
    <Callout variant="note">
      <code>autostart</code> wins. A dependency that sets
      <code>autostart = false</code> stays stopped, and the sheep that
      depends on it starts in its stage as though the edge were satisfied,
      with a warning naming both. <code>depends_on</code> orders what is
      already being started; it does not decide what gets started. An
      explicit statement in one app's own config is not overridden by a
      field in another app's file.
    </Callout>

    <h2>Shutdown runs backwards</h2>
    <p>
      A shepherd going down walks the stages in reverse, so
      <code>web</code> gets its SIGTERM, then <code>api</code>, then
      <code>db</code>. A worker draining its queue drains against a database
      that is still answering. Without ordering both get signalled in the
      same millisecond.
    </p>
    <p>
      A stage's members overlap, so a stage costs the longest
      <code>kill_timeout</code> in it rather than the sum, 1.6 seconds by
      default. Five stages is eight seconds against systemd's default
      <code>TimeoutStopSec</code> of ninety, so <code>shep startup</code>'s
      unit needs nothing extra.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      Dogs are not in the reverse stages. They stop after every sheep,
      because monitoring should outlive what it monitors and a strict
      reverse would kill the bark dog before the flock it reports on.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>shep restart</code> and <code>shep reload</code> against a
      selector matching several sheep walk the stages forward instead,
      dependencies first. Not reverse-stop then forward-start: that sounds
      more correct and behaves worse, since it puts the whole fold down at
      once in the middle.
    </p>

    <h2>What a staged start leaves behind</h2>
    <p>
      Checks shep can make in advance still refuse a batch before anything
      is registered. Once spawning has begun there is nothing to make them
      against: stage 0 is running by the time stage 1 proves unstartable,
      and none of it is rolled back, because rolling back means stopping
      apps that came up fine on a guess about what you wanted. The refusal
      names them instead:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock>{`$ shep start fail.toml
error[spawn_failed]: the daemon reported SpawnFailed: nothing in this batch was registered; 1 of 1 apps cannot start: api: no such file: /private/tmp/shepdocs-boot/app/./not-a-real-binary (this start already has children running and leaves them alone: db)`}</CodeBlock>
    <p>
      <code>shep stop db</code> if you want it down. Nothing after the
      failing stage is attempted in that same invocation.
    </p>

    <h2>Two things this does not do</h2>
    <p>
      Ordering applies to a batch. <code>shep start api</code> on its own
      starts <code>api</code>, immediately, whatever it depends on, because
      there is nothing else in that request to order it against.
    </p>
    <p>
      Promoting a dog fixes the boot window and only the boot window. A
      <code>shep start web</code> typed at noon, while
      <code>log-rotate</code> happens to be down, is a supervision problem.
      Boot ordering has no opinion about it.
    </p>

    <h2>Reading it back</h2>
    <p>
      <code>shep describe</code> lists a sheep's dependencies, which is what
      answers "why did <code>web</code> start six seconds in":
    </p>
    <CodeBlock>{`$ shep describe web
ID  NAME  STATUS  PID    RESTARTS  EXIT  CFG  CPU  MEM   UPTIME  FOLD  SMIT
2   web   online  21907  0         -     -    -    1.2M  0s      -     -

Depends on for web:
  api`}</CodeBlock>
    <p class="fine">
      There is no <code>shep flock</code> column for it. The field names
      other sheep rather than saying anything about this row's own status,
      and that table already drops columns under pressure. Under
      <code>--format json</code> every sheep carries a
      <code>depends_on</code> array.
    </p>

    <h2>Where to go next</h2>
    <div class="next-grid">
      <a href="/docs/first-flockfile" class="next-card">
        <div class="next-title">Your first Flockfile →</div>
        <div class="next-body">
          <code>depends_on</code> in the full field reference, next to
          <code>listen_timeout</code> and <code>readiness_probe</code>.
        </div>
      </a>
      <a href="/docs/dogs" class="next-card">
        <div class="next-title">Dogs →</div>
        <div class="next-body">
          What a dog is, how <code>shep enable</code> writes one into
          <code>shep.toml</code>, and where <code>boot_first_dogs</code>
          sits.
        </div>
      </a>
    </div>
  </article>
</DocsLayout>

